The window bundles SolidJS (MIT), three.js (MIT, https://threejs.org) for the 3D view, and the IBM
Plex fonts (SIL Open Font Licence 1.1). The desktop shell uses Tauri (MIT or Apache-2.0) and the
Rust crates listed in Cargo.lock, each under MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD, ISC, Zlib, MPL-2.0 or Unicode
licences (checked by cargo deny, deny.toml). The clash check uses parry3d (Apache-2.0,
https://parry.rs) and the glam-based maths it brings (MIT or Apache-2.0).

The unit table (crates/core/src/units.rs) is copied from Datum Pipe, which copied it from Datum
Calc (both MIT, same author), with factors from NIST SP 811 (2008).

Every part is generated from a shape rule in code and a dimension table in catalogue/. Pipe
outside diameters and wall thicknesses are the published nominal dimensions of wrought steel pipe
(ASME B36.10M), as listed in Datum Pipe's library. Fitting, flange and valve dimensions are typical
values from manufacturers' public catalogues; every row names its source (catalogue/SOURCES.md).
No standard's tables or text, and no other program's parts library, are reproduced. ASME and
B36.10M are trademarks of the American Society of Mechanical Engineers; Datum Layout is not
affiliated with or endorsed by it.

The piping component file reader in crates/core/src/pcf.rs (module `reader`, used by the tests)
is copied from Datum Pipe (MIT, same author).

Product and file-format names are trademarks of their owners and are used only to describe file compatibility.

The family workflow, ribbon, File page, updater and printing are taken from Datum Plan, by way of
Datum Pipe (both MIT, same author).
